judges, Majority Placement for the next higher position for each couple determined who was ranked higher.  The tiebreakers were then, in order: 1) Number of Majority Placements, 2) Total Ordinals of Majority, 3) Total Ordinals.  Final placement was determined by factored placements.  The placement for the Compulsory Dance #1 was factored by 0.2 (10%), the placement for the Compulsory Dance #2 was factored by 0.2 (10%), the placement for the optional set dance was factored by 0.6 (30%), and the placement for Free Dance was factored by 1.0 (50%).  The sums of the factored placements were then used to determine final placement, with the Free Dance being the tiebreaker."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The defending champions were " [:a {:href "/athletes/81200"} "Oksana Grishchuk"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/96471"} "Yevgeny Platov"] " of Russia. Grishchuk was the star of the show, an aspiring actress always known as Pasha. Since 1994, she had actually changed her name officially to Pasha Grishchuk, saying she was tired of being confused with " [:a {:href "/athletes/80578"} "Oksana Bayul"] ". One thing that did not change was their results, as they were unbeaten since 1994, having won 21 consecutive competitions. The ice dance competition in Nagano was very exciting, …, well, actually, not really. Grishchuk and Platov won easily over their teammates " [:a {:href "/athletes/81578"} "Anzhelika Krylova"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/100158"} "Oleg Ovsyanikov"] ". As an example of how pre-ordained the judging was in ice dancing in that era, the top eight couples ranked in the exact same order, first thru eighth, in all phases of the competition."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Total Factored Placements"] [:th {} "Compulsory Dance 1 Factored Placements"] [:th {} "Compulsory Dance 2 Factored Placements"] [:th {} "Original Set Pattern Dance Factored Placements"] [:th {} "Free 